WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.180
Here is the cast, drawn as Fischer projections.

2
00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:12.200
The pentoses ribose and deoxyribose build your nucleic acids; the only difference is

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:13.600
one missing hydroxyl.

4
00:00:14.060 --> 00:00:19.620
The hexoses glucose, mannose, and galactose differ at just one carbon each: mannose

5
00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.680
at carbon two, galactose at carbon four, which makes them epimers of glucose.

6
00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:30.460
And fructose, off to the side, is the odd one: a ketose, with its carbonyl at carbon

7
00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.180
two. Six sugars carry most of the biological traffic.

8
00:00:34.950 --> 00:00:36.650
Why don't sugars stay straight?

9
00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:41.850
The chemistry is simple: an aldehyde reacts with an alcohol to form a hemiacetal,

10
00:00:42.070 --> 00:00:45.170
and a ketone reacts with an alcohol to form a hemiketal.

11
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:51.250
In a hexose, the sugar's own hydroxyl on carbon-5 swings around and attacks its

12
00:00:51.250 --> 00:00:56.910
carbonyl at carbon-1, closing the molecule into a ring with no enzyme needed, like

13
00:00:56.910 --> 00:00:58.110
a self-closing gate.

14
00:00:58.710 --> 00:01:02.170
That ring closure is exactly what creates the anomeric carbon.

15
00:01:02.983 --> 00:01:04.183
Watch glucose close.

16
00:01:04.763 --> 00:01:09.643
The open chain aldehyde folds so the carbon-5 hydroxyl reaches the carbonyl at

17
00:01:09.643 --> 00:01:11.963
carbon-1, and the ring snaps shut.

18
00:01:12.503 --> 00:01:17.803
The old carbonyl carbon becomes a brand new chiral center, the anomeric carbon, and

19
00:01:17.803 --> 00:01:23.563
it can point two ways: the alpha form has the carbon-1 hydroxyl below the ring, the

20
00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:29.123
beta form above. In solution glucose interconverts between them through the open

21
00:01:29.123 --> 00:01:33.163
chain, settling near one-third alpha and two-thirds beta.

22
00:01:34.033 --> 00:01:36.113
This is the whole chapter on one slide.

23
00:01:36.633 --> 00:01:38.473
Take identical glucose monomers.

24
00:01:39.133 --> 00:01:44.933
Link them with α1-4 bonds and the chain coils into a compact, digestible storage

25
00:01:44.933 --> 00:01:48.673
polymer: starch in plants, glycogen in animals.

26
00:01:49.273 --> 00:01:56.093
Link the very same monomers with β1-4 bonds and the chains lie flat, hydrogen bond

27
00:01:56.093 --> 00:02:00.733
into rigid fibers, and become cellulose, indigestible structure.

28
00:02:01.393 --> 00:02:04.733
One bond geometry, opposite destinies.

29
00:02:05.773 --> 00:02:09.973
The wood in your pencil and the glucose in your blood are the same brick, laid a

30
00:02:09.973 --> 00:02:10.973
different way.

31
00:02:11.367 --> 00:02:12.967
Glycogen's branching is the point.

32
00:02:13.787 --> 00:02:19.847
Alpha-1,4 chains run the length, and an alpha-1,6 branch sprouts about every 10

33
00:02:19.847 --> 00:02:25.887
glucose units. Stack that up and a single liver glycogen molecule can reach 55,000

34
00:02:25.887 --> 00:02:29.907
glucose units with about 2,100 non-reducing ends.

35
00:02:30.447 --> 00:02:31.447
Why does that matter?

36
00:02:31.987 --> 00:02:37.627
Enzymes only pull glucose from the ends, so 2,100 ends means thousands of enzymes

37
00:02:37.627 --> 00:02:42.827
can work at once. Branching is parallel processing for energy: it's what lets you

38
00:02:42.827 --> 00:02:44.967
mobilize glucose fast when you sprint.
